{{also|runup|run-up}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-run up.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|run<,,ran,run> up}}

# {{&lit|en|run|up}}
#: {{ux|en|The small boy '''ran up''' the hill.}}
# To run (towards someone or something); to hasten to a destination.
#: {{ux|en|As I was walking along the road, a man suddenly '''ran up''' to me.}}
#: {{ux|en|The dog '''ran up''' under the table to get his food.}}
# {{lb|en|with ''to''}} To approach (an event or point in time).
#: {{ux|en|We are putting on lots of special attractions as we '''run up''' to Christmas.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To take to a destination or before an authority.
#* '''1924''', Michigan. Supreme Court, ''Michigan Reports'' (volume 226, page 46)
#*: {{...}} and I took him along and '''ran''' him '''up''' to police headquarters.
# To erect hastily, as a building.
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To [[make]] something, usually an item of clothing, very [[quickly]].
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''run''' you '''up''' a skirt for tomorrow evening.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To bring (a [[flag]]) to the top of its [[flag pole]].
#: {{ux|en|Stand quietly while the honor guard '''runs''' the flag '''up'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[string up]]; to [[hang]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} Of a [[bowler]], to run, or [[walk]] up to the [[bowling crease]] in order to [[bowl]] a [[ball]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''runs up'''... and bowls. Smashed away for four runs!}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To rise; to swell; to grow; to increase.
#: {{ux|en|Accounts of goods credited '''run up''' very fast.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Kenilworth|passage=But these, having been untrimmed for many years, had '''run up''' into great bushes, or rather dwarf trees.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[accumulate]] (a [[debt]]).
#: {{ux|en|He '''ran up''' over $5,000 in unpaid bills.}}
# To thrust up, as anything long and slender.
#: {{ux|en|The fence '''runs up''' along the edge of the pasture.}}

